ctures are yet the results are so  different here  but anyway these are in featuring models  in the study they also did a variety of  in-fivo tests  the first one that i want to mention  here is in a projeroid mouse model that  also possesses a reporter  that enables you to detect cells that  have high expression of p16  p16 being a gene that's commonly  upregulated in senescence  and so in these mice they can  effectively use this reporter to  identify senescent cells  and so in this figure here you can see  quite strikingly that the addition of  physician  which they only gave to the mice  intermittently so they gave them  from weeks six to eight and 12 weeks of  age  um this being a project model so they  age faster than normal mice  and even with this intermittent  treatment whereby they were given five  seating in their  child diet so they were having roughly  60 milligrams per kilogram for  5 season per day during these time  periods and at 12 weeks  you can see quite a striking difference  in the luminescence  levels which is how they detect p16  expressing cells  and so this seems to be suggesting  physicians sonolytic potential  and moreover in this graph you can see  that consistently throughout these  different weeks  the number of senescent cells detected  by the  luminescence is always lower in the  flyseason diet compared to the control  diet  and so the thing that they also point  out in this paper is that  even in the weeks where they weren't  getting any physician you still see a  significant difference between the  control and  the thickening diet suggesting that  intermittent treatment  may be effective as a so-called  hit-and-run whereby you can have short  bursts or fiction treatment to get rid  of senescent cells  and then do it again once more if  accumulated and this  comes down to the kinetics of the fact  that physician seems to have  pretty much an immediate impact on the  senescent cells  whereas senescent cells can take much  longer week